> With the Street Openings overlay, there's a slider that lets you control
> what time-slice of the data is shown. It would be really nice to be able to
> specify a mapping like "slider=year", or something like that. With the
> Growth overlay, there are four different years that are clickable; you click
> on a year to see the census data from that year. Conceptually, these things
> let you interactively view a subset of the data. As far as I can tell,
> interactivity is something that can't be described very well with aesthetic
> mappings or with faceting.

To me, these interactive facetting are like facet_time() - lay out the
panels in time rather than space.  This can be a bit of a perceptual
nightmare, but it does have its uses.

> Even the 1811 and 1836 overlay maps could be implemented with something
> this, using a geom_raster for the overlay map. However, instead of mapping a
> data value to the slider, you'd map alpha to the slider.
>
> There are many practical issues with doing this, such as:
> - Grid graphics doesn't have sliders or other interactive components (as far
> as I know)
> - Most export formats don't support interaction (though maybe PDF does?)

Have you seen http://www.stat.auckland.ac.nz/~paul/R/gridSVG/ ? I
don't know whether the underlying architecture is really good enough,
but it's certainly tantalising!

> Even so, it would be nice if the data structures in the new layers package
> could support interactive data presentation, even if the present rendering
> backend doesn't.

Yes, agreed. I think the architecture should (in principle) support
this much more cleanly now you can just implement a new generic
function as an alternative to geom_grob.  It would be interesting to
explore what this would need to be to (e.g.) export to d3.

Hadley

